 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "SALISBURY PLAIN. One of the biggest king penguin colonies on the planet: tens of thousands of pairs on a plain of shingle and meltwater, with the Grace Glacier at its back. Kings do not make nests. Each parent balances the one egg on its feet. Stay back, and keep still, and they will come to inspect you.",
   "plaque": "south_georgia_salisbury",
   "title": "Salisbury Plain"
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "ELEPHANT SEALS. A big bull, a beachmaster, can weigh several tonnes and measure five metres or more. In spring the beaches are full of them, shouting at each other and then lying on each other. The pups drink very rich milk and double their weight in a fortnight or so.",
   "plaque": "south_georgia_seals",
   "title": "Elephant Seals"
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE SANDWICH ISLANDS. A chain of volcanic islands a long way south-east, named by Captain Cook for the Earl of Sandwich, the First Lord of the Admiralty. They are part of this territory, steaming and uninhabited, and home to perhaps a million chinstrap penguins on Zavodovski alone. Nobody has a picnic there.",
   "plaque": "south_georgia_sandwich",
   "title": "South Sandwich Islands"
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "FUR SEALS. They were hunted almost to nothing in the 1800s, then recovered so fully that South Georgia now has most of the world's Antarctic fur seals. In summer a fur seal will defend a patch of beach as if it had a mortgage on it. Give it space.",
   "plaque": "south_georgia_fur_seals",
   "title": "Fur Seals"
  }
 ],
